      <section id="introduction" class="section">
        <p><strong>Bag-on-valve technology</strong> is a modern aerosol packaging system where the product is separated from the propellant inside a multi-layered bag. It works by squeezing the inner bag with compressed nitrogen or air to dispense the product. This matters for aerosol manufacturers because it ensures sterile, 360-degree spraying without propellant contact. This packaging advancement has revolutionized personal care, cosmetics, and particularly pharmaceutical spray formulations. For more details on engineering systems, visit our dedicated <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-technology-and-formulations" target="_top">aerosol technology and formulations</a> landing page.</p>
        <p>Traditional aerosol packaging systems rely on a direct mix of the active liquid concentrate and a liquefied gas propellant within a single chamber. While highly effective for low-viscosity products, this direct contact exposes the active formulation to potential chemical degradation, gas oxidation, and cold-shock temperatures upon dispensing. Bag-on-Valve (BOV) technology completely bypasses these physical limitations. By encapsulating the formulation in an isolated pouch, it prevents any direct contact between the product and the propellant gas, allowing for a purer discharge and absolute protection of oxygen-sensitive active ingredients.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 1 -->
      <section id="bov-system-design" class="section">
        <h2>BOV Aerosol System Design & Components</h2>
        <p>The structural integrity of a BOV aerosol system is determined by a collection of specialized, highly engineered components. Unlike standard aerosol systems, which utilize a simple dip tube, a BOV system integrates a multi-layered bag-on-valve pouch. For an overview of other configurations, see our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/types-of-aerosol-systems-explained" target="_top">types of aerosol systems</a>. This structure is designed to withstand continuous external pressure and prevent gas permeation over multi-year storage cycles. The system consists of the following key elements:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Pressurized Canister:</strong> Typically an extruded aluminum can conforming to <a href="https://www.iso.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ISO</a> standards for packaging. The aluminum can provides a solid pressure barrier that prevents deformation under the stress of compressed gases.</li>
          <li><strong>Multi-layered Laminated Bag:</strong> An aerosol inner bag design consisting of successive layers of polyethylene (PE), oriented polyamide (OPA), aluminum foil, and polyester (PET). The aluminum layer serves as a total barrier against oxygen, moisture, and gas migration.</li>
          <li><strong>Welded Aerosol Valve:</strong> The valve assembly is directly welded to the top of the laminated bag, forming an airtight, hermetic seal. This prevents bypass leakage during pressurized filling operations.</li>
          <li><strong>Actuator and Overcap:</strong> Precision-molded plastic components that define the spray geometry. The actuator is clicked onto the valve stem to enable clean, user-controlled dispensing.</li>
          <li><strong>Propellant Chamber:</strong> The volume between the outer surface of the laminated bag and the inner wall of the aluminum canister, filled with pressurized nitrogen or air.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

