      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="pharmaceutical-applications" class="section">
        <h2>Pharmaceutical Applications & Sterile Aerosol Filling</h2>
        <p>The unique barrier properties of BOV technology make it an ideal choice for sterile medical and pharmaceutical spray formulations. In conventional packaging, keeping a formulation sterile after opening is difficult because outside air enters the container, introducing bacteria and oxygen. In a BOV system, the bag collapses as the product is sprayed, preventing backdraft air from entering. This maintains a sterile seal throughout the product's shelf life. Key pharmaceutical applications include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Sterile Saline Nasal Sprays:</strong> Allowing continuous, comfortable nasal irrigation without the need for chemical preservatives.</li>
          <li><strong>Wound and Burn Care Mists:</strong> Ensuring antiseptic solutions can be applied at any angle without touching sensitive, damaged skin.</li>
          <li><strong>Topical Pain Relief Gels:</strong> Providing precise dosing of active anti-inflammatory creams with rapid absorption and no skin-feel residue.</li>
          <li><strong>Eye Washes and Irrigation:</strong> Offering immediate, continuous flushing of chemical contaminants in emergency medical situations.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary); background: #f0f9ff; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--primary); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Key Insight</strong>
          Sterile integrity in pharmaceutical applications is maintained because the active concentrate never touches the propellant gas, eliminating risk of cross-contamination or oxidation. This allows manufacturers to formulate clean, preservative-free solutions. To see how these formulations are scaled up for commercial production, consult our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/from-lab-to-commercial-launch" target="_top">aerosol R&D and scale-up</a>.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <section id="actuator-selection" class="section">
        <h2>Actuator and Droplet Control in BOV Systems</h2>
        <p>Achieving a consistent, high-performance spray in a BOV system requires careful matching of valve orifices and actuator geometry. Because there is no liquefied propellant to flash-evaporate at the nozzle, atomization relies on the mechanical shear generated within the actuator's internal swirl chambers. Droplet size control is critical to prevent inhalation hazards, especially for topical and nasal drug formulations. Clinical studies on <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubMed</a> highlight the relationship between spray droplet size and respiratory tract penetration.</p>
        <p>To ensure consumer safety, topical and nasal pharmaceutical sprays must maintain a mean droplet diameter above 30 microns. Droplets smaller than 10 microns are considered inhalable and can settle in the deep pulmonary passages, which represents a regulatory and clinical hazard. Engineers use laser diffraction instruments to measure the spray particle size distribution and verify the safety profile of the actuator nozzle.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--amber); background: #fffbeb; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--amber); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Rule of Thumb</strong>
          Droplet sizes for nasal and topical pharmaceutical sprays must remain above 30 microns to prevent inhalation risk, while topical wound sprays can use finer mists. Always pair high-viscosity formulations with actuators that feature mechanical breakup channels.
        </div>
      </section>

