      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <section id="manufacturing-process" class="section">
        <h2>BOV Manufacturing Sequence: Gassing and Crimping</h2>
        <p>The manufacturing process for Bag-on-Valve systems differs significantly from standard aerosol filling lines. For standard lines, you can learn about <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-crimping-gassing-leak-detection-explained" target="_top">aerosol crimping and gassing</a>. For BOV products, the order is reversed. This unique gassing sequence ensures the propellant gas is sealed in the outer chamber before the active concentrate is pumped into the inner bag. The sequence consists of these steps:</p>
        <ol style="margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Valve and Bag Insertion:</strong> The empty, folded laminated bag-on-valve assembly is placed into the clean aluminum canister.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Under-Cup Gassing and Crimping:</strong> Compressed nitrogen or air is injected into the can under the valve cup, and the cup is crimped to form a high-pressure seal. This locks the propellant outside the bag.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Pressure Testing:</strong> The pre-gassed cans pass through a pressure sensor array to identify and reject any containers with low gas charges.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Active Concentrate Injection:</strong> The product liquid is forced through the valve stem at high pressure, expanding the laminated bag within the pressurized gas chamber.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Final Quality Checks:</strong> The filled cans are weighed, passed through leak detection systems, and fitted with actuators and overcaps.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <section id="environmental-compliance" class="section">
        <h2>Environmental and Regulatory Compliance for BOV Packaging</h2>
        <p>From a regulatory standpoint, BOV technology is highly regarded by environmental agencies because it uses non-flammable compressed gases instead of volatile organic propellants. This simplifies compliance with regional Volatile Organic Compound (VOC) limits, which are strictly regulated under global environmental protection frameworks. Organizations like <a href="https://www.bama.co.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BAMA</a> highlight BOV as a leading sustainable technology for reducing carbon footprint in consumer packaging.</p>
        <p>Additionally, the high product evacuation rate of BOV systems (over 99.5%) minimizes chemical waste, ensuring that hazardous formulations are not left inside containers when discarded. The aluminum canisters are fully recyclable, and the elimination of flammable propellants reduces warehouse fire hazard classifications. Read our overview of <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-can-safety-explained" target="_top">aerosol can safety calculations</a> to learn more about pressure ratings. This makes BOV an operationally safer choice for both manufacturers and distributors.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <section id="faq" class="box" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 30px; margin: 40px 0;">
        <h2 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 24px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; color: var(--text-primary);">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px;">
          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What is the primary difference between bag-on-valve and standard aerosol systems?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">The primary difference lies in product separation. In a bag-on-valve system, the liquid formulation is sealed inside a laminated aluminum/polymer bag welded to the valve, while the pressurized propellant gas remains outside in the space between the bag and the can wall. In standard aerosol systems, the propellant and active concentrate are mixed together inside the chamber.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Which propellants are used in Bag-on-Valve technology?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Compressed nitrogen or clean compressed air are the most common propellants used in BOV systems. Because the propellant never comes into direct contact with the product concentrate, manufacturers do not need to use liquefied hydrocarbon gases (like LPG) or organic solvents as carrier diluents, resulting in a cleaner, non-flammable spray system.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Why is BOV technology preferred for sterile pharmaceutical applications?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">BOV technology is preferred because it maintains absolute product sterility throughout its life cycle. The multi-layered laminated bag prevents oxygen, light, and propellant gas from reaching the active formulation. This hermetic seal enables preservative-free formulation chemistry, prevents solvent-driven oxidation, and allows sterile medical-grade liquids or saline washes to be sprayed without air backdraft.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Does Bag-on-Valve allow for continuous 360-degree spraying?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Yes, BOV systems allow for continuous 360-degree spraying, meaning the container can be actuated upright, sideways, or completely upside down. This is possible because the pressurized nitrogen outside the bag squeezes the pouch evenly from all sides to discharge the product, eliminating the reliance on a vertical dip tube.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Are Bag-on-Valve systems compatible with high-viscosity formulations?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Yes, BOV packaging is highly compatible with medium to high-viscosity formulations, such as gels, thick creams, and emulsions. The mechanical pressure exerted by the surrounding compressed nitrogen is capable of pushing viscous substances through the valve, whereas standard aerosols struggle to atomize high-viscosity liquids without adding large volumes of solvents.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="padding-bottom: 8px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What is the environmental profile of Bag-on-Valve aerosol systems?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">BOV systems have a highly favorable environmental profile because they use non-flammable compressed gases like air or nitrogen instead of VOC-heavy liquefied hydrocarbons. The aluminum cans are fully recyclable, and the system enables high product evacuation rates (exceeding 99%), minimizing chemical waste in landfills.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

